# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the Objeck VM, used by tools/tsan/run_tsan.sh
# (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<this file>).
#
# An entry belongs here only when the report is understood and the VM is right.
# Each one says what is reported, why it is not a fault, and what would make the
# entry wrong. A report that is only unexplained does not get an entry: fix it,
# or leave it failing.
#
# How a line matches: "race:NAME" hides a data race when NAME is a function in
# EITHER of the report's two stacks, so a name here must be narrow. "thread:NAME"
# hides a thread-leak report for a thread created under NAME.

# --- 1. A call site patched while another thread interprets it ------------------
# JitCompiler::PatchCallSites and StackInterpreter::CheckAutoJit store a call
# instruction's opcode and operand3 (StackInstr::SetType, SetOperand3) while other
# threads' interpreters read them (GetType, GetOperand3). The two fields are plain
# words on purpose: docs/design/JIT_CALL_SITE_PATCHING.md has the protocol that
# makes every interleaving correct, the ARM64 argument, and the measurement that
# ruled out std::atomic (+1.5% on the interpreter).
# Wrong if: a reader ever needs two fields of an instruction to agree, a patch
# writes more than one word that must be seen together, or anything else starts
# storing through these two setters.
race:StackInstr::SetType
race:StackInstr::SetOperand3

# --- 2. A program thread that was never joined ----------------------------------
# "thread leak": a Thread the program started (ProcessAsyncMethodCall) and did not
# Join before the process ended. Joining is the program's decision: the native
# handle belongs to the Thread object, Thread->Join is what calls pthread_join,
# and the language lets Main return, or a server run for good, without it. At exit
# the VM waits for such threads to finish (StackInterpreter::WaitForThreadsToDrain)
# but cannot join a handle the program may still join. The HTTP and socket
# servers, the actors and the MCP server in the libraries start threads this way,
# and thread_exit_before_start and thread_raise_exit leave theirs unjoined as
# their subject.
# The collector's own threads are created in MemoryManager::CollectMemory and
# CheckPdaRoots and are always joined; this entry does not match them.
# Wrong if: the VM starts creating threads of its own under ProcessAsyncMethodCall.
thread:Runtime::StackInterpreter::ProcessAsyncMethodCall

# --- 3. glibc's time-zone state --------------------------------------------------
# mktime (TrapProcessor::ProcessSetTime2 and the other date traps) calls tzset
# inside glibc, which serializes it with its own lock. glibc is not built with the
# sanitizer, so that lock is invisible and its guarded stores look unguarded.
# Wrong if: the VM itself calls tzset() or writes the TZ variable while threads run.
race:tzset_internal

# --- 4. ONNX Runtime -------------------------------------------------------------
# libonnxruntime.so is a prebuilt third-party library, not instrumented: the tool
# sees its memory accesses through the interceptors but none of its locks, so its
# internal thread pool reports against itself (onnx_concurrency). Nothing in these
# reports is VM or Objeck library code.
# Wrong if: a report names an Objeck frame as well; look at it without this entry.
called_from_lib:libonnxruntime.so
